WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.750
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.340
Whenever we take an image with our camera,
and look at it after an event, we often feel

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:15.110
that many of them are close to perfect, if
only it was less blurry, or the focus distance

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:16.759
was a bit further away.

00:00:16.759 --> 00:00:21.269
But the magic moment is now gone, and there
is nothing to do other than cursing at the

00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:25.039
blurry footage that we're left with when showing
it to our friends.

00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:30.669
However, if we have access to light fields,
we can change some camera parameters after

00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:32.149
the photo was taken.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:36.789
This includes changing the focal distance,
or even slightly adjusting the viewpoint of

00:00:36.789 --> 00:00:37.790
the camera.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.440
How cool is that!

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.420
This can be accomplished by a light field
camera which is also referred to as a plenoptic

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:45.420
camera.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.859
This tries to record not only light intensities,
but the direction of incoming light as well.

00:00:50.859 --> 00:00:55.010
Earlier, this was typically achieved by using
an array of cameras.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.440
That's both expensive and cumbersome.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.999
And here comes the problem with using only
one light field camera: because of the increased

00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:07.200
amount of data that they have to record, current
light field cameras are only able to take

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.290
3 frames per second.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:14.030
That's hardly satisfying if we wish to do
this sort of post-editing for videos.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.579
This work offers a novel technique to remedy
this situation by attaching a standard camera

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:21.170
to this light field camera.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:26.880
The goal is that the standard camera has 30,
so tons of frames per second, but with little

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:32.060
additional information, and the light field
camera, which has only a few frames per second,

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.110
but, with a ton of additional information.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:40.250
If we stitch all this information together
in a smart way, maybe it is a possibility

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.180
to get full light field editing for videos.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.979
Earlier, we have talked about interpolation
techniques that can fill some of the missing

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:49.689
frames in videos.

00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:54.430
This way, we can fill in maybe every other
frame in a footage, or we can be a bit more

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:55.689
generous than that.

00:01:55.689 --> 00:02:01.289
However, if we're shown 3 frames a second,
and we have to create a smooth video by filling

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:06.869
the blanks would almost be like asking an
algorithm to create a movie from a comic book.

00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:09.479
This would be awesome, but we're not there
yet.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.470
Too much information is missing.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.880
This stitching process works with a bit more
information than this, and the key idea is

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:20.890
to use two convolutional neural networks to
fill in the blanks: one is used to predict

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:26.750
flows, which describe the movements and rotations
of the objects in the scene, and one to predict

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.860
the final appearance of the objects.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.060
Basically, one for how they move, and one
for how they look.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.330
And the results are just absolutely incredible.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:40.990
It is also blazing fast and takes less than
a tenth of a second to create one of these

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:42.000
new views.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.550
Here, you can see how the final program is
able to change the focal distance of any of

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:53.090
the frames in our video, or we can even click
on something in the image to get it in focus.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.180
And all this is done after the video has been
taken.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.730
The source code of this project is also available.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:03.120
With some more improvements, this could be
tremendously useful in the film industry,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.500
because the directors could adjust their scenes
after the shooting, and not just sigh over

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.160
the inaccuracies and missed opportunities.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.960
And this is just one of many possible other
applications.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:15.990
Absolutely amazing.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:20.080
If you enjoyed this episode, don't forget
to subscribe to Two Minute Papers and also

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.170
make sure to click the bell icon to never
miss an episode.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

